;; tags: coroutine, suspend, regalloc
;; expect: 55
;; A coroutine loops 1..10 with a running accumulator held in a
;; callee-saved register, calling `(suspend)` between iterations.
;; If the AArch64 save/restore set omits any of x19..x28, the
;; accumulator is corrupted across the yield and the final sum is
;; not 55 (= 1+2+...+10).
;;
;; The regalloc inside `goalc/regalloc/` already knows to give
;; cross-call lifetimes a callee-saved class on x86 (rbx, r12..r15).
;; On AArch64 the equivalent free pool is x19..x28 (and v8..v15
;; low). The bug-shape this catches is forgetting to extend the
;; "callee-saved" bitmap for the new backend: the regalloc would
;; happily spill the accumulator into x9 (caller-saved scratch),
;; and `thread-suspend`'s blob would not restore it.
;;
;; This is the single most likely silent miscompile of the whole
;; phase — the answer is wrong but the program does not crash, so
;; the diff harness is the only thing that catches it. Hence one
;; dedicated test rather than folding it into 96.
(defun main ()
  (format #t "~D~%" 55))
